		{
			"id": "pjVnOb0R",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1281.7119894482335,
			"y": 2593.89600023793,
			"width": 1499.103147743594,
			"height": 160,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0k",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1208271414,
			"version": 209,
			"versionNonce": 1109764842,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775088486700,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Job Managers are leader-based. This means that there is a single Job Manager that is responsible for coordinating the work in the cluster. High availability is achieved by deploying multiple\nJob Managers together and using a quorum-based mechanism (usually ZooKeeper) to elect a leader.\n\nWhen you submit a job to Flink:\n1. The Job Manager receives the application and constructs the execution graph\n2. It allocates tasks to available slots in Task Managers\n3. Task Managers start executing their assigned tasks\n4. The Job Manager monitors execution and handles failures",
			"rawText": "Job Managers are leader-based. This means that there is a single Job Manager that is responsible for coordinating the work in the cluster. High availability is achieved by deploying multiple Job Managers together and using a quorum-based mechanism (usually ZooKeeper) to elect a leader.\n\nWhen you submit a job to Flink:\n1. The Job Manager receives the application and constructs the execution graph\n2. It allocates tasks to available slots in Task Managers\n3. Task Managers start executing their assigned tasks\n4. The Job Manager monitors execution and handles failures",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Job Managers are leader-based. This means that there is a single Job Manager that is responsible for coordinating the work in the cluster. High availability is achieved by deploying multiple Job Managers together and using a quorum-based mechanism (usually ZooKeeper) to elect a leader.\n\nWhen you submit a job to Flink:\n1. The Job Manager receives the application and constructs the execution graph\n2. It allocates tasks to available slots in Task Managers\n3. Task Managers start executing their assigned tasks\n4. The Job Manager monitors execution and handles failures",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "sSjOBIY2",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1279.9303362640242,
			"y": 2890.488967712374,
			"width": 1370.2614021639365,
			"height": 160,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0l",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 2044398454,
			"version": 277,
			"versionNonce": 479088822,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775088486700,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Task Slots and Parallelism\n\nEach Task Manager has one or more task slots, which are the basic unit of resource scheduling in Flink. A task slot is a unit of parallelism, and by default the number of task\nslots is equal to the number of cores on the machine (but this can be overridden to, for instance, use slots to represent chunks of memory or GPUs).\n\nSlots reserve capacity on a machine for jobs and are frequently shared between operators of the same job — i.e. if you allocate 4 slots on a machine, and you have 2\noperators that each need 2 slots, Flink will allocate 4 slots on that machine and run both operators on it.\n",
			"rawText": "Task Slots and Parallelism\n\nEach Task Manager has one or more task slots, which are the basic unit of resource scheduling in Flink. A task slot is a unit of parallelism, and by default the number of task slots is equal to the number of cores on the machine (but this can be overridden to, for instance, use slots to represent chunks of memory or GPUs).\n\nSlots reserve capacity on a machine for jobs and are frequently shared between operators of the same job — i.e. if you allocate 4 slots on a machine, and you have 2 operators that each need 2 slots, Flink will allocate 4 slots on that machine and run both operators on it.\n",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Task Slots and Parallelism\n\nEach Task Manager has one or more task slots, which are the basic unit of resource scheduling in Flink. A task slot is a unit of parallelism, and by default the number of task slots is equal to the number of cores on the machine (but this can be overridden to, for instance, use slots to represent chunks of memory or GPUs).\n\nSlots reserve capacity on a machine for jobs and are frequently shared between operators of the same job — i.e. if you allocate 4 slots on a machine, and you have 2 operators that each need 2 slots, Flink will allocate 4 slots on that machine and run both operators on it.\n",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
