			"text": "Placing job and task managers allows us to distribute work across a cluster, but it doesn't provide any durability guarantees. One of\nthe biggest problems facing stream processing systems (especially stateful ones) is how to ensure that we can recover from failures\nwithout losing data. This is accomplished via Flink's state management system. Let's dive into how it works.",
			"rawText": "Placing job and task managers allows us to distribute work across a cluster, but it doesn't provide any durability guarantees. One of the biggest problems facing stream processing systems (especially stateful ones) is how to ensure that we can recover from failures without losing data. This is accomplished via Flink's state management system. Let's dive into how it works.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Placing job and task managers allows us to distribute work across a cluster, but it doesn't provide any durability guarantees. One of the biggest problems facing stream processing systems (especially stateful ones) is how to ensure that we can recover from failures without losing data. This is accomplished via Flink's state management system. Let's dive into how it works.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "96mc0lKY",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1315.8277809502854,
			"y": 4039.607214854035,
			"width": 1592.0177213268255,
			"height": 240,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b17",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1652936438,
			"version": 292,
			"versionNonce": 2095902326,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775088486700,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "State Backends\n\nWe earlier talked about the abstraction that Flink offers developers for managing state. This API gives each job a way to store state alongside each operator either for the entire job, or for each key.\nThe state itself is stored in a backend, which is a component that manages the storage and retrieval of state.\n\nFlink offers different state backends for different use cases:\n- Memory State Backend: Stores state in JVM heap\n- FS State Backend: Stores state in filesystem\n- RocksDB State Backend: Stores state in RocksDB (supports state larger than memory)\n\nMost of the time you'll prefer using a memory state backend due to its performance, but if you're running an operator which needs to store substantially more state than the available memory you have\noptions for how to page out to disk. Additionally, all of these backends can be configured to store state in remote storage (e.g. S3, GCS, etc.) if you're running Flink in a cloud environment.",
			"rawText": "State Backends\n\nWe earlier talked about the abstraction that Flink offers developers for managing state. This API gives each job a way to store state alongside each operator either for the entire job, or for each key. The state itself is stored in a backend, which is a component that manages the storage and retrieval of state.\n\nFlink offers different state backends for different use cases:\n- Memory State Backend: Stores state in JVM heap\n- FS State Backend: Stores state in filesystem\n- RocksDB State Backend: Stores state in RocksDB (supports state larger than memory)\n\nMost of the time you'll prefer using a memory state backend due to its performance, but if you're running an operator which needs to store substantially more state than the available memory you have options for how to page out to disk. Additionally, all of these backends can be configured to store state in remote storage (e.g. S3, GCS, etc.) if you're running Flink in a cloud environment.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "State Backends\n\nWe earlier talked about the abstraction that Flink offers developers for managing state. This API gives each job a way to store state alongside each operator either for the entire job, or for each key. The state itself is stored in a backend, which is a component that manages the storage and retrieval of state.\n\nFlink offers different state backends for different use cases:\n- Memory State Backend: Stores state in JVM heap\n- FS State Backend: Stores state in filesystem\n- RocksDB State Backend: Stores state in RocksDB (supports state larger than memory)\n\nMost of the time you'll prefer using a memory state backend due to its performance, but if you're running an operator which needs to store substantially more state than the available memory you have options for how to page out to disk. Additionally, all of these backends can be configured to store state in remote storage (e.g. S3, GCS, etc.) if you're running Flink in a cloud environment.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "vQRcXMeI",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1311.18178691226,
			"y": 4404.319001726928,
			"width": 1630.1973004196338,
			"height": 520,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b18",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1646034998,
			"version": 353,
			"versionNonce": 1199037418,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775088486700,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Checkpointing and Exactly-Once Processing\n\nState is awesome except when we need to recover from failure! This is where checkpointing becomes important. Flink's checkpointing mechanism is based on the Chandy-Lamport algorithm for distributed\nsnapshots, which sounds harder to understand than it really is.\n\nRemember with watermarking we're pushing an event through the system that declares \"all events with timestamp ≤ T have arrived\". With checkpointing, we're taking a snapshot of the state of the system at\na given point in time, functionally after all events before the checkpoint have arrived. The job manager takes the lead in this process.\n\nFirst, the job manager initiates a checkpoint by sending a \"checkpoint barrier\" to the sources. This barrier is a special event that flows through the job topology alongside the data. When an operator\nreceives barriers from all inputs, it snapshots its state (serializes it and stores it in its backend). When all operators complete their snapshots, the checkpoint is complete and registered with the job\nmanager.\n\nBy having these periodic checkpoints, we can restore the state of the system from the checkpoint and resume processing from there. When a failure occurs, we stop the world and restore from the\ncheckpoint:\n    1. Failure Detection: The Job Manager notices that a Task Manager is no longer sending heartbeats. It marks that Task Manager as failed.\n    2. Job Pause: The entire job is paused. All tasks, even those running on healthy Task Managers, are stopped. This is important because Flink treats the job as a whole unit for consistency.\n    3. State Recovery: Flink retrieves the most recent checkpoint from the state backend (which could be in memory, filesystem, or RocksDB depending on your configuration).\n    4. Task Redistribution: The Job Manager redistributes all the tasks that were running on the failed Task Manager to the remaining healthy Task Managers. It may also redistribute other tasks to balance\nthe load.\n    5. State Restoration: Each task restores its state from the checkpoint. This means every operator gets back exactly the data it had processed up to the checkpoint.\n    6. Source Rewind: Source operators rewind to their checkpoint positions. For example, a Kafka consumer would go back to the offset it had at checkpoint time.\n    7. Resume Processing: The job resumes processing from the checkpoint. Since the checkpoint contains information about exactly which records were processed, Flink guarantees exactly-once processing\neven after a failure.\n\nWith this entire orchestration, we achieve exactly-once processing. With respect to the stored state, each message is processed exactly once.\n",
			"rawText": "Checkpointing and Exactly-Once Processing\n\nState is awesome except when we need to recover from failure! This is where checkpointing becomes important. Flink's checkpointing mechanism is based on the Chandy-Lamport algorithm for distributed snapshots, which sounds harder to understand than it really is.\n\nRemember with watermarking we're pushing an event through the system that declares \"all events with timestamp ≤ T have arrived\". With checkpointing, we're taking a snapshot of the state of the system at a given point in time, functionally after all events before the checkpoint have arrived. The job manager takes the lead in this process.\n\nFirst, the job manager initiates a checkpoint by sending a \"checkpoint barrier\" to the sources. This barrier is a special event that flows through the job topology alongside the data. When an operator receives barriers from all inputs, it snapshots its state (serializes it and stores it in its backend). When all operators complete their snapshots, the checkpoint is complete and registered with the job manager.\n\nBy having these periodic checkpoints, we can restore the state of the system from the checkpoint and resume processing from there. When a failure occurs, we stop the world and restore from the checkpoint:\n    1. Failure Detection: The Job Manager notices that a Task Manager is no longer sending heartbeats. It marks that Task Manager as failed.\n    2. Job Pause: The entire job is paused. All tasks, even those running on healthy Task Managers, are stopped. This is important because Flink treats the job as a whole unit for consistency.\n    3. State Recovery: Flink retrieves the most recent checkpoint from the state backend (which could be in memory, filesystem, or RocksDB depending on your configuration).\n    4. Task Redistribution: The Job Manager redistributes all the tasks that were running on the failed Task Manager to the remaining healthy Task Managers. It may also redistribute other tasks to balance the load.\n    5. State Restoration: Each task restores its state from the checkpoint. This means every operator gets back exactly the data it had processed up to the checkpoint.\n    6. Source Rewind: Source operators rewind to their checkpoint positions. For example, a Kafka consumer would go back to the offset it had at checkpoint time.\n    7. Resume Processing: The job resumes processing from the checkpoint. Since the checkpoint contains information about exactly which records were processed, Flink guarantees exactly-once processing even after a failure.\n\nWith this entire orchestration, we achieve exactly-once processing. With respect to the stored state, each message is processed exactly once.\n",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Checkpointing and Exactly-Once Processing\n\nState is awesome except when we need to recover from failure! This is where checkpointing becomes important. Flink's checkpointing mechanism is based on the Chandy-Lamport algorithm for distributed snapshots, which sounds harder to understand than it really is.\n\nRemember with watermarking we're pushing an event through the system that declares \"all events with timestamp ≤ T have arrived\". With checkpointing, we're taking a snapshot of the state of the system at a given point in time, functionally after all events before the checkpoint have arrived. The job manager takes the lead in this process.\n\nFirst, the job manager initiates a checkpoint by sending a \"checkpoint barrier\" to the sources. This barrier is a special event that flows through the job topology alongside the data. When an operator receives barriers from all inputs, it snapshots its state (serializes it and stores it in its backend). When all operators complete their snapshots, the checkpoint is complete and registered with the job manager.\n\nBy having these periodic checkpoints, we can restore the state of the system from the checkpoint and resume processing from there. When a failure occurs, we stop the world and restore from the checkpoint:\n    1. Failure Detection: The Job Manager notices that a Task Manager is no longer sending heartbeats. It marks that Task Manager as failed.\n    2. Job Pause: The entire job is paused. All tasks, even those running on healthy Task Managers, are stopped. This is important because Flink treats the job as a whole unit for consistency.\n    3. State Recovery: Flink retrieves the most recent checkpoint from the state backend (which could be in memory, filesystem, or RocksDB depending on your configuration).\n    4. Task Redistribution: The Job Manager redistributes all the tasks that were running on the failed Task Manager to the remaining healthy Task Managers. It may also redistribute other tasks to balance the load.\n    5. State Restoration: Each task restores its state from the checkpoint. This means every operator gets back exactly the data it had processed up to the checkpoint.\n    6. Source Rewind: Source operators rewind to their checkpoint positions. For example, a Kafka consumer would go back to the offset it had at checkpoint time.\n    7. Resume Processing: The job resumes processing from the checkpoint. Since the checkpoint contains information about exactly which records were processed, Flink guarantees exactly-once processing even after a failure.\n\nWith this entire orchestration, we achieve exactly-once processing. With respect to the stored state, each message is processed exactly once.\n",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "B7WxkpYS",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1315.9069520422488,
			"y": 4955.751325918882,
			"width": 1616.7920028715428,
			"height": 60,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#343a40",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b19",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 910887286,
			"version": 190,
			"versionNonce": 1903407030,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775088486700,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "PS: Flink guarantees exactly-once semantics for internal state operations, but this doesn't automatically extend to external systems. For example, when making API calls or writing to external databases,\nyou may still process the same record multiple times in case of failure and recovery. You need to implement idempotent operations or transactional behavior when interacting with external systems to achieve\ntrue end-to-end exactly-once processing.",
			"rawText": "PS: Flink guarantees exactly-once semantics for internal state operations, but this doesn't automatically extend to external systems. For example, when making API calls or writing to external databases, you may still process the same record multiple times in case of failure and recovery. You need to implement idempotent operations or transactional behavior when interacting with external systems to achieve true end-to-end exactly-once processing.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "PS: Flink guarantees exactly-once semantics for internal state operations, but this doesn't automatically extend to external systems. For example, when making API calls or writing to external databases, you may still process the same record multiple times in case of failure and recovery. You need to implement idempotent operations or transactional behavior when interacting with external systems to achieve true end-to-end exactly-once processing.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "fIak5rXJ",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1319.7518579388832,
